// S~S  SimpleSal signal starts  S~S

// [Uno4wifi LED Matrix.note]           search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The UnoR4wifi mesa has a row/column display, accessed through the mesa vendor's ArduinoLEDMatrix.
   Access is available to the fsmDemo App software directly through the ArduinoLEDMatrix C++ code
   and data definitions, or at a simpler level through the mesa "LEDM_" functions specific to UnoR4.
   Before writing to complain, recognize the original intent of ssTEA was in support of MrBlinky.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Individual LEDs on the Matrix are completely controlled by any software using the "modify the LED
   @[row][column] with this value" function.  "Modifying the LED" consists of modifying the memory
   locations being used to describe the state of each LED. To affect the LEDs, use "matrix refresh".
   Separating data preparation from data transfer allows the device interactions to be independent.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Groups of LEDs being updated individually as 0/1 bit values are supported by the mesa software.
   An integer value displayed as a complex image is useful because Humans gain knowledge visually;
   an image such as a "minute hand" on the face of an 1850's clock, or a chevron of 8 large dots.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A meaningful grouping of LEDs is called a "Shape"; each Shape defines on/off values for 4 LEDs.
   The sequence of on/off values is applied to the Matrix to "light" the shape, the reverse of the
   on/off values is applied to the matrix to "clear" the shape.  A related grouping of Shapes is
   called a "Shape Set", which is a sequence of Shapes with an arbitrary number of Shapes defined.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The range of integers 0 to 7, called octal, has a builtin Shape set of 8 clock "hands": Cloctal.
   The software allows for any Shape Set to be displayed in any location, each using any 0-7 input.
   The definition of Shape Sets is contained within this file, separate from SimpleSal or ssTEA.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Each OPTIN build option defined will manipulate the Matrix, in sequence the source code describes
   as a result of the independent OPTIN references.  All of the defined OPTINs are given agency to
   make modifications to the Matrix, in some order, which must be changed here to change the order.
   The overlap of one Shape with another will result in each LED's state being the last value set.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   This is really a choice by the fsmDemo_Application to assume UnoR4 use includes using the Matrix.
   This could be moved to the MesaSelect_UnoR4wifi include file, but then all UnoR4 users include.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #ifdef MESA_ONEOF_mesa_IsUnoR4wifi
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The form of the transfer mechanism is hidden from the ssIO user; there exists a Matrix of LEDs
   that can be addressed with [row, column] syntax to change individual LED values in the Matrix.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The Matrix LED device numbers rows as 0-7 and columns from 0-11, as the viewer sees the Matrix,
   with the UnoR4 positioned before the viewer so that the power LED is visible on the right side.
   The upper left corner is row number 0 and column number 0.  An assumption made is that when a
   BitMap group is displayed and the orientation of the group on the Row R is horizontal, the
   rightmost LED at [R,6] corresponds to Bit0, the leftmost LED at [R,0] corresponds to Bit7.
   This is so strongly visually similar to bit conventions only horizontal BitMaps are supported.
   The result is as desired, with for example a bit pattern rotating right moving to the right and
   an 8-bit value in software or a register in the debugger has the same bit order as displayed.
   find: defining UnoR4 LEDs as Pins for the map of a BitN to a Matrix column number within a group.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #define LEDM_LEDs_Rows  (8)

//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The value written to the cloctal may be directed to either or both (or neither).  This allows the
   N providers of values to the Driver (each running on an independent timer) to update the fields
   associated with the cloctal; each provider was assigned by design.  The design is that the Driver
   is signaled (granted agency) with a Data Event, from a Time Event Cycler and/or Chaser.  If both
   happen to occur at the same Time (both receive a pointer to the Current Time), they will occur in
   an order resulting from ssTEA's method to decide order.  Either can OR in their bit, set values.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   typedef unsigned int    octalNumber_t;
//

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
